# Project 07: Static NAT Configuration for Public Web Server Access


## Objective

To configure Static NAT and allow an external device to access an internal web server using a public IP address.

## Network Design

Internal Network:

* 192.168.20.0/24

Router LAN Interface:

* 192.168.20.1

Router WAN Interface:

* 209.165.200.230

Web Server:

* 192.168.20.100

External Device:

* 209.165.200.10

External Device Gateway:

* 209.165.200.226

## Topology


Web Server
192.168.20.100
       |
       |
Router
G0/0: 192.168.20.1
G0/1: 209.165.200.230
       |
       |
External Device
209.165.200.10


## Key Configuration

### Configure Router Interfaces

interface g0/0

ip address 192.168.20.1 255.255.255.0

ip nat inside

no shutdown

interface g0/1

ip address 209.165.200.230 255.255.255.224

ip nat outside

no shutdown


### Configure Static NAT


ip nat inside source static 192.168.20.100 209.165.200.230


## Verification Commands


show ip nat translations


show ip nat statistics


show running-config


## Testing

From the external device:


ping 209.165.200.230




## Results

* The private web server was successfully mapped to a public IP address.
* External users were able to access the internal web server.
* NAT translation entries were successfully created and verified.
* End-to-end connectivity was confirmed through testing.

## Skills Demonstrated

* Static NAT Configuration
* Cisco Router Administration
* Public and Private IP Address Translation
* Web Server Publishing
* Network Connectivity Testing
* Troubleshooting NAT Issues

## Lessons Learned

This project demonstrated how Static NAT creates a permanent mapping between a private IP address and a public IP address. It provided practical experience in publishing internal services to external users while maintaining private addressing within the local network.
